Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Introduction.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

Socrates wrote nothing, so his character comes down to us through two students who knew him: Plato and Xenophon. The Stoics took him as their model of the wise man, and Epictetus held him up again and again as proof that a human being can live by virtue alone.

This list gathers the moments in their writings that show Socrates practicing the four cardinal virtues. It draws on the works in the Arete corpus: Plato's Apology and Gorgias (Jowett's translation) and Xenophon's Memorabilia (Dakyns's translation). References to Plato use the standard page numbers, like Apology 28e; references to Xenophon give book, chapter, and section, like Memorabilia I.2.1. Several of the most famous episodes come from dialogues not yet in the corpus, and they are listed separately at the end.

Xenophon closes his book with his own summary of the man: so just that he never harmed anyone, so self-controlled that he never chose the pleasant over the better, and so wise that he never misjudged better from worse (Memorabilia IV.8.11).$q$, 170, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Courage$q$, 1002, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Courage.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- He held his post in battle. Socrates served as a soldier at Potidaea, Amphipolis, and Delium and stayed where his commanders stationed him, facing death. He argued he must hold his post in philosophy just as firmly, where the god had placed him (Apology 28e).

- He refused to stop philosophizing, even to save his life. Offered release on condition that he stop questioning people, he said he would obey the god rather than the Athenians and would not change his ways even if he had to die many times (Apology 29c to 30c).

- He stood alone against the Assembly at the trial of the generals. When the Athenians wanted to try the generals of Arginusae together, which was illegal, Socrates was the only presiding officer to oppose it, though the orators threatened to impeach and arrest him (Apology 32b; Memorabilia I.1.18, IV.4.2).

- He defied the Thirty Tyrants over Leon of Salamis. Ordered with four others to bring in Leon to be executed, Socrates simply went home, knowing it might cost him his life (Apology 32c to 32d; Memorabilia IV.4.3).

- He mocked the Thirty to their faces. While they were killing citizens, he remarked that a herdsman who kept shrinking his herd would be called a poor herdsman, and so should a ruler who kept shrinking the city (Memorabilia I.2.32).

- He questioned Critias and Charicles when they tried to silence him. Summoned and forbidden to talk with young men, he calmly asked them to define their terms, until Charicles lost his temper (Memorabilia I.2.33 to 38).

- He would not beg for his life. He refused to weep before the jury or parade his children, saying it would disgrace him and the city and ask the judges to break their oath (Apology 34c to 35d). Xenophon adds that he could easily have been acquitted by a little flattery, and chose to obey the law and die (Memorabilia IV.4.4).

- He proposed a reward instead of a penalty. Asked to propose his own punishment, he said a public benefactor deserved free meals in the Prytaneum, the honor given to Olympic victors (Apology 36d to 37a).

- He refused to fear death. To fear death is to think you know what you do not; no one knows whether it is the greatest good. He knew only that doing wrong and disobeying a better is evil (Apology 29a to 29b).

- He met his sentence without bitterness. He told the jury no evil can happen to a good man in life or in death (Apology 41c to 41d). Xenophon reports he bore his last days with a cheerfulness that astonished his friends (Memorabilia IV.8.2).$q$, 436, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Courage > Modern examples to strive for$q$, 1003, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Courage > Modern examples to strive for.
Verification: interpretive: application or guidance, not a report of what the sources say.
Do not present this section as what any ancient author said.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- Be the one who says no in the room. When a meeting drifts toward something unfair or dishonest, say so plainly, even if you are the only one, as Socrates was at the trial of the generals.

- Refuse the unethical order quietly. If a boss or client asks you to falsify a report, mislead a customer, or cut a corner that could hurt someone, decline and go home, the way Socrates did with Leon of Salamis. You do not need a speech.

- Stay at your post when it gets hard. Keep showing up for the difficult obligation: the aging parent, the struggling marriage, the project everyone else has abandoned.

- Tell the truth to people with power over you. Give an honest assessment to a manager, a board, or a client who would rather hear something else.

- Do not beg for approval. In a performance review, a conflict, or an online argument, make your case honestly and let it stand without flattery or self-abasement.

- Face the diagnosis, the layoff, or the loss without pretending to know it is the worst thing. Take the next right step and leave the rest open, as Socrates did about death.

- Keep your practice when it costs you. Whatever your version of philosophy is, whether honesty, faith, or principle, do not drop it just because it has become inconvenient or unpopular.$q$, 225, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Self-discipline and temperance$q$, 1004, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Self-discipline and temperance.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- He mastered his appetites and endured hardship. Xenophon calls him the most self-controlled of men in matters of sex and food, able to endure winter cold and summer heat and every kind of toil, and trained to need so little that he always had enough (Memorabilia I.2.1).

- He lived on almost nothing. Antiphon the sophist mocked him for eating poor food, wearing one cloak winter and summer, and going barefoot. Socrates answered that hunger is the best sauce, that he trained his body to bear whatever came, and that the greatest pleasure is knowing you are becoming better and making better friends (Memorabilia I.6.2 to 10).

- He ate and drank only for need. He ate only as much as he could eat with pleasure and came to meals hungry enough that appetite was his seasoning (Memorabilia I.3.5 to 6).

- He took no money for his company. He called those who charged for their conversation sellers of themselves, and valued his freedom to talk with whomever he chose (Memorabilia I.2.5 to 7, I.6.5).

- He warned against the pull of beauty. When Critobulus kissed a handsome youth, Socrates told Xenophon that a kiss is more dangerous than a tarantula's bite and advised him to run from such beauty without looking back. For himself, Xenophon says, he was so well armed that abstaining from the most beautiful was easier for him than for others to abstain from the plainest (Memorabilia I.3.8 to 15).

- He rebuked Critias in public. When Critias, later the most violent of the Thirty, kept pursuing young Euthydemus, Socrates told him it was unworthy of a free man, and when that failed, mocked his desire before the company. Critias never forgave him (Memorabilia I.2.29 to 31).

- He taught self-control as the foundation of virtue. He asked what man could learn or practice anything good without self-control, and called the self-controlled man free and the slave to pleasure the least free of all (Memorabilia I.5, IV.5).

- He poked fun at gluttony. At shared dinners he teased the man who ate his relish without bread, and warned that mixing many dainties in one mouthful spoils them all (Memorabilia III.14).

- He never chose the pleasant over the better. Xenophon's final judgment: in all his life he never preferred what was sweeter to what was better (Memorabilia IV.8.11).$q$, 384, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Self-discipline and temperance > Modern examples to strive for$q$, 1005, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Self-discipline and temperance > Modern examples to strive for.
Verification: interpretive: application or guidance, not a report of what the sources say.
