- Put the phone down. Treat the endless scroll the way Socrates treated the tempting kiss: the pull is stronger than it looks, so keep your distance rather than trusting yourself to resist up close.

- Eat when hungry and stop when satisfied. Let appetite be the seasoning, and skip the snacking, the second helping, and the drink you do not really want.

- Train for discomfort on purpose. Take the cold shower, the hard workout, the long walk in bad weather, so that hardship does not rule you when it comes uninvited.

- Want less. Wear the same few clothes, keep the old car running, and measure wealth by how little you need, as Socrates answered Antiphon.

- Do not sell your time or judgment to the highest bidder. Turn down the job, client, or sponsorship that would make you say what you do not believe.

- Choose the better over the sweeter. Pick the book over the show, the early morning over the late night, and the hard conversation over the easy avoidance.

- Hold friends to a standard, kindly. When a friend is pursuing something that will hurt them or someone else, say so, as Socrates did with Critias, even if it costs you the friendship.$q$, 202, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Wisdom$q$, 1006, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Wisdom.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- He knew that he did not know. When the oracle at Delphi told Chaerephon that no one was wiser than Socrates, he set out to prove it wrong. He questioned politicians, poets, and craftsmen and found that each thought he knew things he did not. He concluded that his only wisdom was knowing the limits of his knowledge, and that the god meant human wisdom is worth little or nothing (Apology 21a to 23b).

- He kept examining, whatever it cost him. He called this questioning his service to the god, and it earned him enemies and poverty, but he would not give it up (Apology 22e to 23c).

- He held the unexamined life not worth living. Rather than go quietly into exile, he told the jury that daily talk about virtue is the greatest good for a man, and that life without such examination is not worth living (Apology 38a).

- He called himself the city's gadfly. He compared himself to a gadfly sent by the god to wake a great but sluggish horse, reminding each citizen to care for his soul before his wealth (Apology 30a to 30e).

- He humbled a would-be statesman. Young Glaucon, not yet twenty, was eager to lead Athens. Socrates gently asked him about the city's revenues, expenses, army, silver mines, and grain supply, until Glaucon saw he knew none of it and should learn before he led (Memorabilia III.6).

- He urged a capable man into public life. Charmides was able but afraid to speak before the Assembly. Socrates showed him he already outargued its leaders in private, and told him not to be ignorant of himself (Memorabilia III.7).

- He brought a proud reader to know himself. Euthydemus had collected the books of poets and philosophers and thought himself wise. Through questions about justice, Socrates led him to see he could not define what he thought he knew, and pointed him to the Delphic command to know yourself (Memorabilia IV.2).

- He held that virtue is knowledge. He did not separate wisdom from self-control, and said justice and every other virtue is wisdom, since all fine actions are done by those who know (Memorabilia III.9.4 to 5).

- He fitted his teaching to each person. He tamed those proud of their natural gifts by showing that the best natures most need training, and treated the rich, the shy, and the arrogant differently (Memorabilia IV.1).

- He limited study to what is useful. He encouraged enough geometry to measure land and enough astronomy to tell the seasons, but warned against studies that consume a life and yield nothing for living well (Memorabilia IV.7).$q$, 433, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Wisdom > Modern examples to strive for$q$, 1007, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Wisdom > Modern examples to strive for.
Verification: interpretive: application or guidance, not a report of what the sources say.
Do not present this section as what any ancient author said.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- Say "I don't know" out loud. In a meeting, with your kids, or with a client, admit the limits of your knowledge instead of bluffing. It is the beginning of Socratic wisdom.

- Check your own confidence. Before you post, argue, or decide, ask the questions Socrates asked Glaucon: do I actually know the numbers, the history, the facts behind my opinion?

- Examine your day. Keep a journal or an evening review asking what you did well, where you fell short, and what you will do differently, so that your life is examined and not just lived.

- Ask questions instead of lecturing. When someone you are mentoring is headed the wrong way, ask them to walk you through their reasoning, the way Socrates led Euthydemus to see for himself.

- Encourage the capable but timid. Notice the colleague or student who knows more than they let on, and push them forward, as Socrates did with Charmides.

- Learn what is useful for living well. Spend your study time on what makes you a better person, parent, and citizen, not on trivia or endless information you will never use.

- Be the gadfly your community needs. Kindly and persistently remind the people around you of what matters more than money and status.$q$, 207, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Justice: how he treated people$q$, 1008, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Justice: how he treated people.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- He never wronged anyone. Near death he said he knew he would be remembered as one who never wronged any man or made anyone worse, but always tried to make his companions better (Memorabilia IV.8.10).

- He showed justice by deeds, not definitions. When the sophist Hippias demanded his definition of justice, Socrates answered that he showed it in what he did, and asked whether Hippias had ever seen him bear false witness, inform on anyone, or stir up quarrels among friends or strife in the city. Hippias admitted he had not (Memorabilia IV.4.10 to 11).

- He would rather suffer wrong than do it. Against Polus and Callicles he argued that doing injustice is worse than suffering it, and escaping punishment worst of all (Gorgias 469b to 469c, 474b to 479e).

- He kept the law even when it killed him. He obeyed the laws in private life and in office, refused unlawful orders from the Assembly and the Thirty, and at his trial would do nothing outside the law, preferring to die lawfully than live by breaking it (Memorabilia IV.4.1 to 4).

- He taught for free and stayed poor. He never took a fee from anyone, rich or poor, and his poverty was his witness that he spent his life on the city's good (Apology 31b to 31c, 33a to 33b; Memorabilia I.2.5 to 7).

- He helped a friend feed a household of fourteen. When civil war left Aristarchus with fourteen sisters, nieces, and cousins to support and no income, Socrates advised him to let them work at the skills they had. The women became productive and happy, and the household grew fond of him again (Memorabilia II.7).

- He found honest work for a friend fallen on hard times. He advised Eutherus, who had lost his property abroad and was living by manual labor, to find steady work as a steward before his strength gave out (Memorabilia II.8).

- He protected Crito from blackmailers. He suggested Crito take on Archedemus, an honest but poor speaker, to defend him from informers, and Archedemus became a loyal friend (Memorabilia II.9).

- He urged friends to help each other. He persuaded Diodorus that helping their struggling friend Hermogenes was worth more than saving a runaway slave (Memorabilia II.10).

- He taught a son to honor his mother. When his own son Lamprocles complained of his mother's harsh temper, Socrates showed him how much she had done for him and that no one would trust a man ungrateful to his parents (Memorabilia II.2).

- He reconciled two quarreling brothers. He told Chaerecrates to make the first kind move toward his brother Chaerephon, just as the hands and feet were made to help each other (Memorabilia II.3).$q$, 447, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Justice: how he treated people$q$, 1009, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Justice: how he treated people.
Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- He asked people to weigh their worth as friends. He urged his companions to ask what they were worth to their friends, and taught that good friends are the most valuable possession (Memorabilia II.4 to 6).

- He checked anger at a slave. When a man beat his servant as lazy and greedy, Socrates asked whether he had ever considered which of the two deserved the beating more (Memorabilia III.13.4).

- He forgave his judges. He told them he was not angry with those who condemned him, and asked only that they correct his sons as he had corrected them if they cared for money before virtue (Apology 41d to 42a).$q$, 109, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Justice: how he treated people > Modern examples to strive for$q$, 1010, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon (version 1). Section: Justice: how he treated people > Modern examples to strive for.
Verification: interpretive: application or guidance, not a report of what the sources say.
Do not present this section as what any ancient author said.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

- Let your record speak. Aim to be someone about whom no one can say you lied about them, spread gossip, or set friends against each other, the test Socrates put to Hippias.

- Take the loss rather than cheat. Accept losing the deal, the argument, or the promotion before winning it by deceit or cruelty.

- Help a friend solve the real problem. When someone is struggling with money, work, or family, think with them about practical steps, as Socrates did for Aristarchus and Eutherus, not just offer sympathy.

- Make the introduction. Connect the person who needs help with the person who can give it, the way Socrates brought Crito and Archedemus together.

- Honor your parents, even the difficult ones. Remember what they gave you before you list what they got wrong, as Socrates taught Lamprocles.

- Make the first move toward reconciliation. Call the sibling, old friend, or colleague you have fallen out with. Do not wait for them to go first.

- Watch how you treat those under you. Before you lose your temper with an employee, a waiter, or your kids, ask whether you are the one who needs correcting.

- Give your knowledge freely. Mentor, teach, and share what you know with people who cannot pay you back.

- Forgive the people who wronged you. Let go of resentment toward those who have harmed you, and still ask the people around you to hold your children to a high standard.$q$, 238, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
