Neither Socrates nor the Stoics made rules for every activity. They gave a way of judging, and then applied it to whatever life brought. This section runs the ordinary activities of modern life through that judgment. The verdicts are Practice (build it into your life), In moderation (fine within limits, dangerous past them), and Avoid (it works against virtue by its nature).$q$, 62, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$A Socratic life in modern terms > Six tests$q$, 1012, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
