Every verdict below comes from one or more of these questions.

1. Better or sweeter? Does this make me better, or only feel good? Socrates never chose the sweeter over the better (Memorabilia IV.8.11).

2. Master or slave? Can I take it or leave it, or does it pull me? The Stoics called the person ruled by pleasure the least free of all (Memorabilia IV.5).

3. Does it keep my judgment clear? Reason is the one thing wholly ours. Whatever clouds it gives away the self (Epictetus; Seneca, Letters 83).

4. Is it up to me? Put effort into what I control, my choices, and not into what I don't, like others' opinions or the news (Epictetus, Handbook 1).

5. How does it treat people? Justice asks whether an activity uses, harms, or neglects someone.

6. Would I do it with someone I respect watching? Seneca advised imagining Cato looking on (Letters 11). Pick your own: Socrates, your wife, your children.$q$, 161, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$A Socratic life in modern terms > The body$q$, 1013, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
