|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Food|In moderation|Eat when hungry and stop when satisfied; plain food, with hunger as the sauce (Memorabilia I.3.5 to 6, I.6.5)|Grazing all day, eating from boredom or stress, living for the next meal|
|Alcohol|In moderation|A drink with friends or a meal, never past clear judgment; Socrates drank but was never drunk|Getting drunk, which Seneca called voluntary madness (Letters 83); drinking to cope; keeping it in reach every night|
|Recreational drugs|Avoid|A mind you never hand over to a substance; medicine taken as prescribed is a different matter|Using anything to get high, escape, or numb; it fails the third test by design|
|Coffee and caffeine|In moderation|A cup that serves your work; now and then go without, to prove it doesn't own you, as Seneca practiced going without comforts (Letters 18)|Not being able to function or be civil without it; caffeine to override the sleep you owe your body|
|Exercise|Practice|Train regularly for strength and endurance so hardship finds you ready; Socrates urged young men to stay fit (Memorabilia III.12) and trained himself against cold and toil|Neglecting the body, or the reverse: making fitness a vanity, an obsession, or a performance for others; Seneca advised short, simple exercise that leaves time for the mind (Letters 15)|
|Sleep|Practice|Enough sleep to keep your judgment sharp; get up when it's time, since you were made to act, not lie in bed (Meditations V.1)|Staying up scrolling; sleeping late as a habit; treating rest as a reward instead of a duty|
|Sex|Within marriage|Faithfulness and self-control; Epictetus advised restraint and warned against both indulgence and preaching at others (Handbook 33)|Treating people as objects; infidelity; letting desire decide for you, which Socrates compared to a tarantula's bite (Memorabilia I.3.12)|
|Pornography|Avoid|Keep your distance, as Socrates told Xenophon to run from beauty without looking back (Memorabilia I.3.13)|Watching it at all; it enslaves by habit, trains you to use people, and works on impressions you never examine|
|Cold, discomfort, and hardship|Practice|Seek some voluntarily: the cold shower, the fast, the long walk in bad weather, so that hardship doesn't rule you when it comes uninvited|Arranging life so you are never uncomfortable, then collapsing when you are|$q$, 355, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
