Socrates gave the rule that settles most of modern eating: avoid any food that tempts you to eat when you are not hungry, and any drink that tempts you to drink when you are not thirsty (Memorabilia I.3.6). Musonius Rufus, who wrote more on food than any other Stoic, called eating the hardest pleasure to fight, because it tempts us every day and usually twice a day. He favored cheap, plentiful food, eaten for nourishment rather than pleasure (Lectures XVIIIA and XVIIIB). Epictetus added that spending much time on eating is a sign of a dull mind, since the body should be tended in passing (Handbook 41). Eating animals is treated separately below as an open question.

|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Plain, whole food|Practice|Simple meals of grains, vegetables, cheese, and honey, as Musonius advised, with hunger as the sauce|Making every meal an event; needing flavor and variety to eat at all|
|Ultra-processed and snack food|Avoid|Food that stops being appealing once you are full|Food engineered to make you eat past hunger, exactly what Socrates warned against|
|Set meals|Practice|Eat at meals, on purpose, then stop|Grazing through the day; the kitchen as a place you drift to|
|Eating in front of a screen|Avoid|Eat at a table, with others when you can, paying attention to the food|Eating without tasting or noticing when you have had enough|
|Sugar and dessert|Avoid as a habit|An occasional sweet at a shared table|A nightly dessert you would miss; sweetness as a reward you think you have earned|
|Fasting|Practice, as training|Days of going without so that hunger never frightens you, as Seneca kept (Letters 18)|Fasting for appearance or pride; making it a performance|
|Fine dining and feasts|In moderation|A good meal with friends, enjoyed and then forgotten|Spending money and attention chasing the next great meal; judging a day by what you ate|
|Diet obsession|Avoid|Eat simply and think about it little (Handbook 41)|Fads, tracking every bite, supplements as a hobby, food as identity; the same fault as gluttony, too much of the mind given to the stomach|$q$, 336, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$A Socratic life in modern terms > Attention and screens$q$, 1015, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
