The Stoics had no phones, but they knew the problem. Epictetus taught that the one thing wholly ours is how we use our impressions, and every screen is a machine for supplying impressions faster than we can examine them.

|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Social media|Only as a tool|Use it as a tool with a purpose: post the work, answer the message, close the app. Marcus noted how much time you gain by not looking to see what your neighbor said or did (Meditations IV.18)|Scrolling to fill every gap; posting for approval; measuring your worth in likes; comparing your life to other people's highlights|
|Online arguments|Avoid|Make your case once, honestly, and let it stand, as Socrates did with the jury|Arguing with strangers to win; outrage as entertainment; saying online what you would never say face to face|
|Television and streaming|Avoid as entertainment|Watch only what you choose deliberately because it teaches, then turn it off; Epictetus saw dramas as people suffering over externals|Watching by default, bingeing into the night, letting autoplay decide; Seneca warned that he came home from the crowds and shows more cruel and more greedy (Letters 7)|
|News|In moderation|Know enough to be a responsible citizen and neighbor, then act on what is up to you|Constant checking; anxiety and anger over things you cannot affect; letting the news cycle set your mood|
|The phone itself|In moderation|Keep it out of the bedroom, off the dinner table, and away during conversation|Reaching for it on reflex; checking it while someone is talking to you; the first and last thing you touch each day|
|Video games|Avoid, except as play with your children|Play that is really care for someone else|Hours lost alone; games designed to hook you with rewards and purchases|
|Reading|Practice|Read the wise of old, as Socrates did with his friends, unrolling their books together and taking what was good (Memorabilia I.6.14)|Reading only to seem learned, the "inn" Epictetus warned of (Discourses II.23); hoarding books unread, like Euthydemus|$q$, 325, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$A Socratic life in modern terms > Money and work$q$, 1016, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
