|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Work|Practice|Do your role well, whatever it is; Epictetus taught that each role, parent, citizen, worker, carries its own duties|Coasting; doing the minimum; or the reverse, letting work swallow family, health, and thought|
|Earning money|In moderation|Earn honestly and enough; the Stoics counted wealth as a preferred thing, never a good in itself|Chasing money for its own sake; letting income decide what you believe or say|
|Spending|In moderation|Buy what serves a real need; want less, as Socrates did|Shopping for comfort or status; buying things to impress people you don't know|
|Debt|Avoid where possible|Live within your means so you stay free|Borrowing for pleasures and appearances; the Stoics prized freedom, and debt sells it|
|Gambling|Avoid|Take your risks on work and people, where effort matters|Betting for the thrill or to get rich; it hands your outcome to chance and trains the itch|
|Giving and generosity|Practice|Help friends practically, as Socrates did for Aristarchus; share what you know without charge|Giving for credit or praise; lending to control people|
|Business dealings|Practice honesty|Tell the whole truth about what you sell; keep your word when it costs you|Misleading customers, fine print, or promises you know you won't keep; winning a deal by deceit, which Socrates held worse than losing it|$q$, 205, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
