|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|YouTube and online video|Only as a tool|Watch with a purpose, then stop; put up barriers, such as no app on the phone, so the choice is made before the pull begins|Rabbit holes, autoplay, and "one more"; the pull is designed to be stronger than your intention in the moment|
|Watching life through a phone|Avoid|Be present at the recital, the game, the parade; let the memory be yours|Recording instead of watching; your child looking up to see your phone instead of your face|
|AI tools|In moderation|Use AI to question, teach, and test you, as Socrates taught by asking; keep doing your own thinking and writing|Letting AI think, write, or decide for you; outsourcing the effort that forms judgment|
|Podcasts and audiobooks|In moderation|Learning on a drive or a run, followed up by reading the source itself|Constant background input so you are never alone with your thoughts|
|Streaks, trackers, and metrics|In moderation|Use the numbers as a tool for training; the progress that counts is in character|Chasing a perfect streak; letting an app or a score decide whether the day was good; giving up after one broken day|
|Habits|Practice|Every habit chosen by reason and built by practice; Epictetus taught that each act strengthens the habit it belongs to, so each refusal makes the next easier (Discourses II.18)|Habits formed by drift rather than choice; excusing them as just how you are|
|Alcohol in social settings|Avoid relying on it|Show up clear; if a drink feels necessary to be at ease with people, that ease is the thing to train|Drinking to get through a party, a family gathering, or awkwardness; teaching children that adults need it|
|Voluntary hardship and extreme challenges|Practice, with an examined motive|Marathons, fasts, cold, and sparring as training of the will, so hardship finds you ready; ask whether it makes you better toward others|Hard things for image or ego; becoming short-tempered or neglecting family in the name of training|
|Combat sports and martial training|In moderation|Training that teaches humility, discipline, and calm under pressure|Using the skill to intimidate; pride in being able to hurt people|$q$, 345, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
