None of these is a grave vice on its own. Each is a place where the body acts without your choosing, which makes them the best ground for training attention. The method is the same for all of them: never feed the habit once, and count the days, as Epictetus told the angry man to count the days he was not angry (Discourses II.18).

|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Nail biting|Avoid|Catch the hand at the mouth and refuse it; keep nails short so there is nothing to bite, as you would keep wine out of the house|Letting boredom, worry, or hard thinking move your hands for you|
|Hitting snooze|Avoid|Rise at the first alarm; you were made to act, not to stay under the blankets (Meditations V.1)|Bargaining with the alarm; starting the day with a small defeat|
|Complaining|Avoid|Say nothing about what is not up to you; act on what is|Complaint as conversation; every complaint assents to the judgment that something outside your will is bad|
|Swearing and coarse talk|Avoid|Plain, clean speech; Epictetus advised against coarse talk and silence when others fall into it (Handbook 33)|Cursing from habit or anger; coarse speech makes coarse conduct easier|
|Interrupting|Avoid|Hear the whole answer before you speak; Socrates's method depended on it|Waiting for your turn instead of listening; finishing other people's sentences|
|Nicotine and vaping|Avoid|A body and mind that need nothing to feel steady|Dependence by design; it fails the master or slave test outright|
|Filler words and fidgeting|Practice correcting|Notice and cut them; harmless in themselves, they are good practice in attention|Treating them as too small to bother with|
|Posture|Practice|Stand and sit with attention, the body carried as something you govern|Slumping by default for hours at a desk or on a couch|$q$, 285, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$A Socratic life in modern terms > Home, family, and difficult people$q$, 1021, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
