|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Raising your voice at your children|Avoid|Discipline by self-control, which children learn by watching it; the sage never assents to the angry impression, and on examination there is usually nothing to be angry about|Yelling as discipline, which teaches self-control by losing it; letting exhaustion speak for you|
|When a child breaks or loses something|Practice|Treat it as a lesson, not a catastrophe; Epictetus taught that if you love a jug, remember it is a jug, and you won't be upset when it breaks (Handbook 3)|Fury over an object; making a child feel less valuable than the thing lost|
|Helping family and friends|Practice|Serve willingly and keep no count; refuse only when helping would be unjust or would make the person worse, as Socrates refused the Thirty|Saying yes, then keeping a silent ledger of what you are owed|
|Extended family and visits|Practice|Patience and presence, treating the difficulty as training; Socrates said living with Xanthippe prepared him for anyone, as a horse trainer who masters a spirited horse can handle the rest (Diogenes Laertius II.37). The drain comes from your judgments about the visit, not the visitors; if something needs saying, say it plainly|Withdrawing into sullen silence; or losing yourself to everyone else's expectations|
|Complainers and draining people|Practice patience|Listen with compassion, remember that no one does wrong willingly, and do not absorb it|Joining the complaint; judging them; carrying it home|
|Expecting people to be better than they are|Avoid|Expect each morning to meet ungrateful, meddling people, as Marcus did, and treat them well anyway (Meditations II.1)|Shock and bitterness when friends and family fall short|
|Possessions, theft, and loss|Practice detachment|Hold things lightly; Epictetus pointed out that the angry victim of a theft values the same thing the thief does (Discourses I.18)|Anger over things taken or damaged; defining yourself by what you own|
|Wealth beyond need|Avoid|Enough, and then generosity|Piling up wealth far past need while others go without|$q$, 315, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
